So, we change the corresponding inequality symbols above as follows:\n\n$$\ny\\le 4,\\,y\\le 4x+1\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\le 4,\\,y\\le 4x+1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313066 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36fa0f2f-3812-521a-aca3-900556be84ff" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Non-Rectangular Sets Using Set-Builder Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "efaedcbb-db41-597e-be57-70272676fbe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313068} {:answer/id #uuid "73cedf0c-5740-540a-933f-49ccc34134c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313069} {:answer/id #uuid "812add9c-8f14-552a-84b9-949817d586df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{Z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313070} {:answer/id #uuid "b88ef0d8-6c49-5ff4-87f0-fb6d2ed26a3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efaedcbb-db41-597e-be57-70272676fbe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313068} :answer-field/id #uuid "07d1f6a2-93d9-514c-bf85-a09b7bf2386e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313067} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "409eff4d-9f84-5267-81a9-f82d5d5135ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313073} {:answer/id #uuid "2b8bd5c5-10a2-5118-b406-fb0a0a72bc6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ne " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313074} {:answer/id #uuid "d9d98e5e-4713-5649-a5b0-197e1fcbcadb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313075} {:answer/id #uuid "9a9687c1-9eb2-5fd0-961e-aaae34f1f0c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313076} {:answer/id #uuid "c2a9425a-5680-5179-a324-99565a2c0e17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2a9425a-5680-5179-a324-99565a2c0e17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313077} :answer-field/id #uuid "11debab5-76b5-5bef-9909-a32347b8bccd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7026035-86e3-5abe-a75f-990ad273e2b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-181691" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041529/assets/6affe267a93d1dab16de3dc153fddbdb165bab831790f7387dcbd29354ebb63a.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *below* the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny<-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is not included in the set (the curve is dashed), the inequality is strict.\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y<-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36fa0f2f-3812-521a-aca3-900556be84ff" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Non-Rectangular Sets Using Set-Builder Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71a7f34f-5b40-545a-bfe9-9b55fda6dd80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71a7f34f-5b40-545a-bfe9-9b55fda6dd80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313080} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b8601b2-1afc-537f-a796-ab7867755b52" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313079} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59685f75-e8f2-51c0-a015-46e411bd7c31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59685f75-e8f2-51c0-a015-46e411bd7c31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313082} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca7caad9-afa5-509d-814e-5d12ff61deff" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313081} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ea5ff0b-c8f9-5207-bfe5-b97d68be645b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ea5ff0b-c8f9-5207-bfe5-b97d68be645b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313084} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1453974-9515-59b7-a97b-11a0a067b4e4" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "776a3516-03ea-5a65-aa0b-94e7bab490df" :question/math-academy-id "q-181696" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041529/assets/5cd989164ddda8a542098548f401a055e085943e35d95a63fe47b132f723d8ce.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n${y}^{2}$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$64$\n\n$}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=2$ and vertical radius $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=64.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *outside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point outside the region, say, $(0,5).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n16(0)^{2}+4(5)^{2}\\,>\\,64.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}>64.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=64$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\ge 64.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\ge 64}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313085 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9f0da82-f79b-5056-ac99-b401eb1630c7" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Rectangular Sets Using Set-Builder Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7478a8db-a471-58a4-8c81-4f07290a0323" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313087} {:answer/id #uuid "fc1f3c80-782d-5910-b1d6-3b03d6e4bfe0" :answer/type {: