ca606d840d0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313222} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b807db8-7eae-5042-8877-ca9e4368b55d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "29d1034a-32f8-58de-bfb9-996dde4c8c01" :question/math-academy-id "q-182024" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041529/assets/5adfe417126264290b9b49776a535e8ca27521a6bb13806df13bffe2807402fe.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,-1}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in Z,y\\in Z,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in Z\\times Z={Z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *to the right* the line $x=-1.$ For those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\nx>-1\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that some points in our set lie *on* the line. We include these points by changing the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\ge -1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {Z}^{2}\\,:\\,x\\ge -1}\n$$"}]}
